# The variable list, as a file you can copy:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored; this file is committed, so nothing in it may be a
# real secret. The one value it does carry is the local container's, which is
# published to loopback and guards nothing. Every other secret below is left
# blank on purpose — a committed placeholder is a value somebody keeps. Next.js reads `.env.local`
# automatically; `drizzle.config.ts` and `src/db/migrate.mts` read it
# explicitly, because they are separate processes that load nothing on their own.
#
# The schema this mirrors is `src/lib/env-schema.mts`, and it is checked when the
# server starts rather than when something first reads a value — a missing or
# malformed variable is a startup error naming the variable, not a TypeError in a
# request handler an hour into a deploy. A variable added to one of these two
# files and not the other is half a change.
#
# README.md, under "Environment variables", says where each value comes from in
# each environment.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only on the server, and never sent to a browser. `src/server/env.ts` is
# `server-only`, so a Client Component that imports it fails the build.
#
# Anything secret belongs in this section and nowhere else.

# Postgres. Local Postgres comes from docker-compose.yml — `npm run db:up`
# starts it, and the value below is the one it is configured with, so this line
# works unedited.
#
# These are the committed development credentials, deliberately. The database
# holds no real data and is published to loopback only. Never reuse the password
# anywhere reachable from off the machine.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://capexwise:capexwise_local_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/capexwise

# The origin this instance answers on, with no trailing slash. Better Auth builds
# the OAuth callback from it — `APP_URL` + `/api/auth/callback/google` — and
# Google refuses a callback that is not on its client's redirect-URI list, so a
# wrong value here fails at the provider with a message about a URI mismatch and
# nothing about us.
#
# Set it everywhere except a Vercel preview. A preview's hostname is generated
# per branch, so there it is derived from `VERCEL_BRANCH_URL` (below) when left
# unset (#32). Nowhere else is it derived: a fallback would turn a missing value
# into a silent redirect to the wrong host rather than a startup error.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Signs session cookies, and encrypts the OAuth tokens stored in `accounts`.
# At least 32 characters, checked at boot.
#
# **Generate your own — never copy this line.** A shared development secret in a
# public repository is a signing key anybody can forge a session with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Rotating it signs everybody out and makes every stored provider token
# undecryptable, which is the right response to a leak and a bad surprise
# otherwise.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# The Google OAuth client. Required: ADR-0004 makes Google the only way to sign
# in during v0, so an instance without these is one nobody can enter.
#
# Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials → Create credentials →
# OAuth client ID → Web application. Add one Authorised redirect URI per
# environment, each of them `<that environment's APP_URL>/api/auth/callback/google`.
# The consent screen can stay in Testing while the app is personal; add the
# Google accounts that need in as test users.
#
# The secret is a real secret. Local development gets its own client — never the
# production one.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The keys that seal building access codes before they reach the database
# (ADR-0008). Comma-separated `<version>:<key>` entries. The highest version
# seals new codes, and every listed version can open old ones. Checked at boot.
#
# **Generate your own — never copy one.** One key is enough to start:
#
#   node -e "console.log('1:' + require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Rotating means adding a version rather than replacing one, `2:<new>,1:<old>`,
# and dropping the old version only once nothing sealed under it remains.
# ADR-0008 has the steps. A code sealed under a key you no longer hold is gone,
# which is survivable locally and the reason production's is kept outside
# Vercel as well.
ACCESS_CODE_KEYS=

# The bearer token that calls the nightly demo reset, `/api/cron/demo-reset`
# (#34, ADR-0011). Optional: unset, the route answers 404 and the reset is off.
# Set it to reset the local demo with `npm run demo:reset`, which calls the
# route on a running dev server. At least 32 characters:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# In production it is the Vercel project's own `CRON_SECRET`, which Vercel Cron
# sends as `Authorization: Bearer` on its own.
# CRON_SECRET=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set by Vercel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel sets these on every deployment, and nobody sets them by hand. They are
# listed so the schema and this file still agree. Leave them unset locally: set
# here, they would make a laptop behave like a preview.
#
# Which environment this is: `production`, `preview` or `development`. On a
# preview the sign-in page says sign-in is off instead of offering Google.
# VERCEL_ENV=
#
# The preview branch's hostname, without the scheme. It is read only on a
# preview, and only when `APP_URL` is unset, to derive `APP_URL`.
# VERCEL_BRANCH_URL=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only by the integration suite, never by the server. It is deliberately a
# separate variable from DATABASE_URL rather than a reuse of it, because the two
# mean opposite things: one names a database to keep, the other names one the
# suite empties before every single test.
#
# Leave it unset. Unset, the suite uses the local compose database with `_test`
# appended and creates it on first run, which is what `npm run db:up` provides.
# Set it only to point the suite at a different server — and note that
# `src/test/db.ts` refuses any value whose database name does not end in `_test`,
# so a copy of the line above will be rejected rather than truncated.
#
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://capexwise:capexwise_local_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/capexwise_test


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backup
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only by `npm run db:backup`, never by the server. The nightly workflow
# sets both itself (`.github/workflows/backup.yml`, ADR-0010), so these are here
# for running it by hand. `docs/runbooks/backup-and-restore.md` has the command.
#
# The source is the database to back up. The restore target is a database the
# command drops and recreates on every run, and it refuses any whose name does
# not end in `_restore`. Both lines below are the local compose database.
#
# BACKUP_SOURCE_URL=postgresql://capexwise:capexwise_local_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/capexwise
# BACKUP_RESTORE_URL=postgresql://capexwise:capexwise_local_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/capexwise_restore


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are none yet, and the section is here so the first one lands in the
# right half of the file rather than next to the database password.
#
# A variable named `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is not merely conventionally public: the
# compiler inlines its value into the client bundle as a literal, so it is
# published to every browser and stays published in every cached build. A secret
# that acquires the prefix cannot be un-published by removing it. Nothing in the
# Server section above may be renamed into this one to make it reachable from a
# Client Component — pass the value down from a Server Component instead.
#
# `src/lib/env-schema.mts` refuses to start the server if a server variable
# carries the prefix.
